> AI is a tool, just like other tools we use. And it's clearly a=0D
> useful one.=0D
=0D
I agree. I am a 26-year-old college student, not a kernel developer or=0D
an AI researcher, so I want to be precise about why I am replying.=0D
=0D
I have been reading Just for Fun and have reached page 129. One theme I=0D
took from it - perhaps colored by my own experience - is that intense=0D
technical focus can create something important while crowding out the=0D
rest of life. Work on the attached Continuity-Lag hypothesis made me=0D
recognize that tendency in myself. I do not want this question to become=0D
my identity or pretend that I have solved a systemic problem.=0D
=0D
I also related to your account of Finnish military service. I served in=0D
the U.S. Marine Corps. My shorthand is that I loved the clowns but hated=0D
the circus. Shared hardship and camaraderie leave plenty of stories, and=0D
"embrace the suck" helped us stay positive: get through it together,=0D
learn from it, and know it would make a good story later.=0D
=0D
> The solution is to make sure those LLM tools help maintainers instead=0D
> of just causing them pain.=0D
=0D
My own use of AI has led me to a related conclusion. The more I ask it=0D
to write code, the more obvious it becomes that I must understand the=0D
code well enough to judge it. If I cannot recognize a bad design, an=0D
unsafe assumption, or a plausible-looking error, asking a model to code=0D
does not remove my responsibility; it only hides the gap. AI can teach,=0D
compare, and suggest, but it cannot replace experience earned by coding.=0D
=0D
I am building a local environment to learn Linux, Git, Python, and SQL,=0D
with a small local model offering inline suggestions. The goal is help=0D
practicing and verifying my thinking, not outsourcing it.=0D
=0D
The attachment is a pre-analysis hypothesis packet, not an empirical=0D
result. It records what I observed, turns the idea into something=0D
falsifiable, states rival explanations, and says when the hypothesis=0D
should be rejected.=0D
=0D
My point is not that the sky is falling or that I found an answer for=0D
everyone. At most, this is a tornado watch, not a warning: some=0D
conditions deserve examination, but I am not claiming disaster is=0D
underway. AI can accelerate useful work and existing failures alike;=0D
people remain responsible for deciding what it is used to create.=0D
=0D
I saw something from my limited point of view and wanted to say so. I am=0D
sharing the work so people with relevant expertise can break it, narrow=0D
it, disregard it, or build on it. The best outcome is that nothing=0D
dramatic happens, the evidence shows my concern was overstated, and the=0D
hypothesis is rejected or narrowed.=0D
=0D
For my part, I am putting the question down and having fun with computers=0D
again: finishing college, doing meaningful work, making my laptop and=0D
handheld my own, 3D-printing things, playing games, going to concerts,=0D
and making room for relationships. I would rather keep learning and=0D
building than remain stressed trying to solve AI's problems for everyone.=0D
=0D
AI-assisted drafting disclosure: I dictated the substance of this=0D
message in several passes. OpenAI Codex transcribed, organized,=0D
shortened, and edited those thoughts into a plain-text draft. I reviewed=0D
the revisions and retained final control over the wording, claims, and=0D
decision to send. The experiences and views are mine, as is=0D
responsibility for the message.=0D
=0D
Regards,=0D
Peter=0D
